defmodule <%= @module %>.ApiClients do
  @moduledoc """
  The runtime of the generated API Connector clients
  (`<%= @module %>.ApiClients.*`, one module per Bubble API Connector group,
  one function per call).

  Every client function takes `params`, a map or keyword list of the call's
  non-private parameters (a missing one is not sent; in a JSON body it is
  `""` inside a string and `null` as a value), and `opts`:

    * `:env` - a map of environment variables that takes precedence over
      the configured ones (tests pass stubs here)
    * `:base_url` - replaces the call's scheme, host and port
    * `:follow_redirects` - follow redirects (default `false`; see
      "Redirects"), at most `:max_redirects` (default 5)
    * `:max_body_bytes` - the largest response body read, decompressed
      (default <%= @max_body_bytes_text %>); a longer one is `{:body_too_large, limit}`
    * `:deadline` - the whole call, retries and redirects included, in
      milliseconds (default <%= @deadline_text %>); then `:deadline_exceeded`
    * `:max_retry_delay` - the longest wait before a retry, whatever a
      `retry-after` header asks (default <%= @max_retry_delay_text %> ms)
    * `:allow_private_addresses` - allow loopback, private and link-local
      addresses (default `false`; see "Addresses")
    * any `Req` option (`:plug`, `:retry`, `:receive_timeout`, …) but
      `:redirect`, `:into` and `:decode_body`, which this runtime sets

  Options also come from the application environment, per group module,
  under the call's own options:

      config :<%= @app %>, <%= @module %>.ApiClients.SomeGroup,
        base_url: "https://sandbox.example.com",
        receive_timeout: 10_000

  The defaults are a <%= @receive_timeout %> ms receive timeout, a <%= @connect_timeout %> ms
  connect timeout and a <%= @deadline_text %> ms deadline for the whole call. A call
  Bubble uses as an action (every workflow step) is **never retried**; a
  GET Bubble uses as data is retried (at most <%= @max_retries %> times) only on a
  refused connection, a 429 or a 503, waiting what `retry-after` asks, at
  most `:max_retry_delay`. A `:retry` in the configuration replaces this
  (do not set `:retry_delay` with it). Redirects are not followed. No
  request is compressed on the way back unless the server insists: a
  gzip or deflate body is inflated to at most `:max_body_bytes`.

  ## Addresses

  The host of a call is Bubble's, or an environment variable's, never a
  step's or a page's value (a call whose host has a public parameter is
  not generated). Even so, before each request (and each redirect) the
  host is resolved and refused (`{:blocked_address, _}`) when any address
  is loopback, private, link-local (cloud metadata), carrier-grade NAT,
  multicast or reserved, unless the owner allows it: the `:base_url`'s
  origin (e.g. `http://localhost:4001` configured for development), or
  `allow_private_addresses: true`. The address is checked, then Req
  resolves it again to connect: a DNS answer that changes in between
  (rebinding) is not caught.

  **Credentials go wherever the request goes.** The call's secrets (keys,
  private headers and parameters, redacted literals) are sent to the
  `:base_url` given in the options or the configuration when there is one:
  point it only at a host you trust with them.

  ## Redirects

  A 3xx response is an error (`{:status, status, body}`) unless
  `follow_redirects: true` is given (in the options or the configuration).
  Then a redirect to the same origin (scheme, host and port) keeps the
  request's headers and authentication; a redirect to another origin, or
  from HTTPS to HTTP, drops **every** header and the authentication
  (they may hold secrets), and a 307/308 redirect to another origin (which
  would resend the body) is refused (`{:redirect_refused, status,
  location}`). 301, 302 and 303 continue as a GET without a body.

  A function returns `{:ok, body}` for a 2xx response (decoded JSON, a
  typed struct when the call returns a known API Connector type, text, or
  nil for an empty response), or `{:error, reason}`: `{:status, status,
  body}`, a `<%= @module %>.ApiClients.MissingEnvError` (a secret it needs is
  not set: nothing was sent), `{:unknown_parameters, keys}`,
  `:invalid_parameters` (params neither a map nor a keyword list),
  `{:decode, reason}` or a transport exception.

  A call with Bubble's "Include errors in response and allow workflow
  actions to continue" returns `{:ok, result}` for any response: on a 2xx
  its body with `returned_an_error` false, otherwise `returned_an_error`
  true and `error`'s `status_code`, `status_message` and `body` (the
  response body as text), as Bubble's result has them. A transport error
  (a timeout, a refused connection) is still `{:error, exception}`.

  The clients never log (`retry_log_level: false`). An error never holds
  the request's headers, authentication or secrets; with "Include errors
  in response" the error body is the result, as in Bubble, and an API
  that echoes a request's secrets in its error body puts them there.
  Finch's telemetry events carry each request's headers: do not attach
  handlers that record them. There is no rate limiting (Bubble has none).

  ## Secrets

  Bubble's private values, and every literal of a request that is not
  structure (path words, versions, media types), are never in the code:
  they are read from these environment variables, which
  `config/runtime.exs` reads into the application environment
  (`config :<%= @app %>, <%= @module %>.ApiClients, env: %{name => value}`) when the
  app boots. A call whose variables are not set (or empty) is refused with
  a `<%= @module %>.ApiClients.MissingEnvError` naming them, before anything is
  sent: a migrated app fails closed until its owner sets them.
  `mix bubble.api_env` lists the ones that are missing, `.env.example`
  names them all (`.wtf/api_clients.json` lists them too).
  A variable holding a header or parameter whose name Bubble does not send
  holds `Name: value` (a header) or `name=value` (a parameter). A host
  variable (`<GROUP>_HOST`) holds an API host, or part of one, that is
  account-specific; it is shared by the group's calls and not needed when
  the group's `:base_url` is configured.

<%= @env_doc %>
  """

  @app :<%= @app %>

  defmodule MissingEnvError do
    @moduledoc """
    A call needs environment variables that are not set (or are empty):
    it was refused before anything was sent. `names` are the variables,
    never their values. Set them where the app runs (`config/runtime.exs`
    reads them); `mix bubble.api_env` lists every missing one.
    """
    defexception names: []

    @impl true
    def message(%__MODULE__{names: names}),
      do:
        "API Connector call refused: set the environment variable(s) " <>
          Enum.join(names, ", ") <> " (read in config/runtime.exs)"
  end

  @env_names <%= @env_names %>

  @defaults [
    receive_timeout: <%= @receive_timeout %>,
    connect_options: [timeout: <%= @connect_timeout %>],
    retry: false,
    max_retries: <%= @max_retries %>,
    retry_log_level: false,
    redirect: false,
    compressed: false
  ]

  # This runtime's own options (see the moduledoc), with their defaults.
  @own [
    follow_redirects: false,
    max_redirects: 5,
    max_body_bytes: <%= @max_body_bytes %>,
    deadline: <%= @deadline %>,
    max_retry_delay: <%= @max_retry_delay %>,
    allow_private_addresses: false
  ]

  @doc false
  @spec params(map() | keyword(), [atom()]) :: {:ok, map()} | {:error, term()}
  def params(params, keys) when is_map(params) or is_list(params) do
    params = Map.new(params)

    case Map.keys(params) -- keys do
      [] -> {:ok, params}
      unknown -> {:error, {:unknown_parameters, Enum.sort(unknown)}}
    end
  end

  def params(_params, _keys), do: {:error, :invalid_parameters}

  @doc false
  @spec env(keyword(), [String.t()], module() | nil, [String.t()]) ::
          {:ok, %{String.t() => String.t()}} | {:error, term()}
  def env(opts, names, group \\ nil, hosts \\ []) do
    overrides = Keyword.get(opts, :env, %{})
    configured = configured_env()

    # Host variables are needed only without a configured base URL.
    base_url? =
      Keyword.has_key?(opts, :base_url) or
        (group != nil and Keyword.has_key?(Application.get_env(@app, group, []), :base_url))

    names = if base_url?, do: names, else: names ++ hosts

    values =
      Map.new(names, fn name ->
        {name, present(Map.get(overrides, name)) || present(Map.get(configured, name))}
      end)

    case for({name, nil} <- values, do: name) do
      [] -> {:ok, values}
      missing -> {:error, %MissingEnvError{names: Enum.sort(missing)}}
    end
  end

  @doc """
  The environment variables every generated call may read, by name (from
  `config/runtime.exs`); `missing_env/0` those not set.
  """
  @spec env_names() :: [String.t()]
  def env_names, do: @env_names

  @doc "The environment variables that are not set (or empty), sorted."
  @spec missing_env() :: [String.t()]
  def missing_env do
    configured = configured_env()
    for name <- @env_names, present(Map.get(configured, name)) == nil, do: name
  end

  # Only what config/runtime.exs put in the application environment: never
  # the system environment directly, never the export.
  defp configured_env do
    case Application.fetch_env(@app, __MODULE__) do
      {:ok, config} when is_list(config) ->
        case Keyword.get(config, :env) do
          env when is_map(env) -> env
          env when is_list(env) -> Map.new(env)
          _ -> %{}
        end

      _ ->
        %{}
    end
  end

  defp present(value) when is_binary(value) and value != "", do: value
  defp present(_), do: nil

  @doc false
  @spec request(module(), keyword(), map()) :: {:ok, term()} | {:error, term()}
  def request(group, opts, request) do
    config = Application.get_env(@app, group, [])
    {config_base, config} = Keyword.pop(config, :base_url)
    {opts_base, opts} = opts |> Keyword.delete(:env) |> Keyword.pop(:base_url)
    owner_base = opts_base || config_base
    base = owner_base || request.base
    chosen = Keyword.merge(config, opts)

    {own, options} =
      @defaults
      |> Keyword.merge(@own)
      |> Keyword.merge(chosen)
      |> Keyword.split(Keyword.keys(@own))

    own = Keyword.merge(@own, own)

    # A call Bubble uses as data (a GET) is retried on a refused
    # connection, a 429 or a 503, waiting at most :max_retry_delay; an
    # action never, unless the configuration says so.
    retry =
      if Map.get(request, :retry, false) and not Keyword.has_key?(chosen, :retry),
        do: [retry: retry_fun(own[:max_retry_delay])],
        else: []

    options =
      options
      |> Keyword.merge(retry)
      |> Keyword.merge(
        method: request.method,
        url: String.trim_trailing(base, "/") <> request.path,
        params: entries(request.query),
        headers: entries(request.headers),
        decode_body: false,
        redirect: false,
        into: collect(own[:max_body_bytes])
      )
      |> Keyword.merge(body(request.body))
      |> Keyword.merge(auth(request.auth))

    ctx = %{
      response: request.response,
      wrap?: Map.get(request, :wrap_errors, false),
      limit: own[:max_body_bytes],
      # Only what the owner configured may be a private address: the base
      # URL's origin, or every address with :allow_private_addresses (and
      # a Req.Test plug, which never connects).
      guard: %{
        trusted: owner_base && origin(URI.parse(owner_base)),
        allow?: own[:allow_private_addresses] == true or Keyword.has_key?(options, :plug)
      }
    }

    redirects = if own[:follow_redirects], do: own[:max_redirects], else: 0
    within(own[:deadline], fn -> send_request(options, redirects, ctx) end)
  end

  @redirects [301, 302, 303, 307, 308]

  defp send_request(options, redirects, ctx) do
    with :ok <- check_address(Keyword.fetch!(options, :url), ctx.guard) do
      case Req.request(options) do
        {:ok, %Req.Response{body: {:too_large, limit}}} ->
          {:error, {:body_too_large, limit}}

        {:ok, %Req.Response{status: status} = resp} when status in @redirects and redirects > 0 ->
          with {:ok, options} <- redirect(options, resp),
               do: send_request(options, redirects - 1, ctx)

        {:ok, %Req.Response{} = resp} ->
          with {:ok, body} <- content(resp, ctx.limit), do: respond(resp.status, body, ctx)

        {:error, exception} ->
          {:error, exception}
      end
    end
  end

  defp respond(status, body, ctx) when status in 200..299,
    do: decode(body, ctx.response, if(ctx.wrap?, do: %{"returned_an_error" => false}, else: %{}))

  defp respond(status, body, %{wrap?: true} = ctx), do: wrapped_error(status, body, ctx.response)
  defp respond(status, body, _ctx), do: {:error, {:status, status, body}}

  # The whole call, retries and redirects included, within `ms`.
  defp within(ms, fun) do
    task =
      Task.async(fn ->
        try do
          fun.()
        catch
          _kind, _reason -> {:error, :crashed}
        end
      end)

    case Task.yield(task, ms) || Task.shutdown(task, :brutal_kill) do
      {:ok, result} -> result
      {:exit, _reason} -> {:error, :crashed}
      nil -> {:error, :deadline_exceeded}
    end
  end

  defp retry_fun(max_delay) do
    fn _request, response_or_exception ->
      case response_or_exception do
        %Req.Response{status: status} = resp when status in [429, 503] ->
          {:delay, min(Req.Response.get_retry_after(resp) || 1_000, max_delay)}

        %Req.TransportError{reason: :econnrefused} ->
          {:delay, min(1_000, max_delay)}

        _ ->
          false
      end
    end
  end

  # The response body, at most `limit` bytes: anything longer is dropped
  # as it arrives (`{:too_large, limit}`).
  defp collect(limit) do
    fn {:data, data}, {req, resp} ->
      case resp.body do
        {:too_large, _} = too_large ->
          {:halt, {req, %{resp | body: too_large}}}

        body when byte_size(body) + byte_size(data) > limit ->
          {:halt, {req, %{resp | body: {:too_large, limit}}}}

        body ->
          {:cont, {req, %{resp | body: body <> data}}}
      end
    end
  end

  # The body as sent, decompressed (gzip, deflate) to at most `limit`
  # bytes; another encoding is an error.
  defp content(%Req.Response{body: body} = resp, limit) do
    case Enum.map(Req.Response.get_header(resp, "content-encoding"), &String.downcase/1) do
      encoding when encoding in [[], ["identity"]] -> {:ok, body}
      [gzip] when gzip in ["gzip", "x-gzip"] -> inflate(body, 31, limit)
      ["deflate"] -> inflate(body, 15, limit)
      _ -> {:error, {:decode, :content_encoding}}
    end
  end

  @doc false
  # Inflates `data` (`window` 31: gzip, 15: zlib) to at most `limit` bytes.
  @spec inflate(binary(), integer(), non_neg_integer()) :: {:ok, binary()} | {:error, term()}
  def inflate(data, window, limit) do
    z = :zlib.open()

    try do
      :ok = :zlib.inflateInit(z, window)
      inflate_loop(z, :zlib.safeInflate(z, data), [], 0, limit)
    rescue
      ErlangError -> {:error, {:decode, :content_encoding}}
    after
      :zlib.close(z)
    end
  end

  defp inflate_loop(z, {status, out}, acc, size, limit) do
    size = size + IO.iodata_length(out)

    cond do
      size > limit -> {:error, {:body_too_large, limit}}
      status == :finished -> {:ok, IO.iodata_to_binary(Enum.reverse([out | acc]))}
      true -> inflate_loop(z, :zlib.safeInflate(z, []), [out | acc], size, limit)
    end
  end

  # --- addresses ----------------------------------------------------------------------

  @doc false
  # Refuses an address the owner did not configure that is loopback,
  # private, link-local (cloud metadata included), carrier-grade NAT,
  # multicast or reserved, after resolving the host: a call never reaches
  # the app's own network unless its base URL says so.
  @spec check_address(String.t(), map()) :: :ok | {:error, term()}
  def check_address(url, guard) do
    uri = URI.parse(url)

    cond do
      guard.allow? ->
        :ok

      guard.trusted != nil and origin(uri) == guard.trusted ->
        :ok

      true ->
        case resolve(uri.host) do
          {:ok, addresses} ->
            if Enum.any?(addresses, &blocked?/1),
              do: {:error, {:blocked_address, :private}},
              else: :ok

          :error ->
            {:error, {:blocked_address, :unresolved}}
        end
    end
  end

  defp origin(%URI{scheme: scheme, host: host, port: port}),
    do: {scheme, host && String.downcase(host), port}

  defp resolve(nil), do: :error

  defp resolve(host) do
    name = String.to_charlist(host)

    case :inet.parse_address(name) do
      {:ok, address} ->
        {:ok, [address]}

      {:error, _} ->
        addresses =
          for family <- [:inet, :inet6],
              {:ok, found} <- [:inet.getaddrs(name, family)],
              address <- found,
              do: address

        if addresses == [], do: :error, else: {:ok, addresses}
    end
  end

  @doc false
  @spec blocked?(:inet.ip_address()) :: boolean()
  def blocked?({a, b, c, _}) do
    a in [0, 10, 127] or a >= 224 or (a == 100 and b in 64..127) or (a == 169 and b == 254) or
      (a == 172 and b in 16..31) or (a == 192 and b == 168) or {a, b, c} == {192, 0, 0} or
      (a == 198 and b in 18..19)
  end

  # IPv4-mapped, -compatible and NAT64 addresses are their IPv4 address.
  def blocked?({0, 0, 0, 0, 0, prefix, x, y}) when prefix in [0, 0xFFFF],
    do: x == 0 or blocked?(v4(x, y))

  def blocked?({0x64, 0xFF9B, 0, 0, 0, 0, x, y}), do: blocked?(v4(x, y))

  def blocked?({first, _, _, _, _, _, _, _}),
    do: Bitwise.band(first, 0xFE00) == 0xFC00 or Bitwise.band(first, 0xFFC0) == 0xFE80 or
          Bitwise.band(first, 0xFF00) == 0xFF00

  defp v4(x, y), do: {Bitwise.bsr(x, 8), Bitwise.band(x, 0xFF), Bitwise.bsr(y, 8), Bitwise.band(y, 0xFF)}

  # Bubble's result of a call that includes errors in its response.
  defp wrapped_error(status, body, response) do
    term = %{
      "returned_an_error" => true,
      "error" => %{
        "status_code" => status,
        "status_message" => status_message(status),
        "body" => if(is_binary(body), do: body, else: "")
      }
    }

    case response do
      {:json, decoder, _list?} -> decoder.(term)
      _ -> {:ok, term}
    end
  end

  defp status_message(status) do
    Plug.Conn.Status.reason_phrase(status)
  rescue
    ArgumentError -> ""
  end

  # The next request of a redirect. Another origin (or a downgrade to
  # HTTP) gets no headers and no authentication; a 307/308 to another
  # origin, which would resend the body, is refused.
  defp redirect(options, %Req.Response{status: status} = resp) do
    case Req.Response.get_header(resp, "location") do
      [location | _] ->
        from = options |> Keyword.fetch!(:url) |> URI.parse()
        to = URI.merge(from, location)
        same_origin? = {to.scheme, to.host, to.port} == {from.scheme, from.host, from.port}

        options =
          options
          |> Keyword.put(:url, URI.to_string(to))
          |> Keyword.delete(:params)

        options =
          if status in [301, 302, 303],
            do: options |> Keyword.put(:method, :get) |> Keyword.drop([:json, :form, :body]),
            else: options

        cond do
          same_origin? -> {:ok, options}
          status in [307, 308] -> {:error, {:redirect_refused, status, URI.to_string(to)}}
          true -> {:ok, options |> Keyword.put(:headers, []) |> Keyword.delete(:auth)}
        end

      [] ->
        {:error, {:status, status, resp.body}}
    end
  end

  defp entries(entries), do: for({name, value} <- entries, value != nil, do: {name, value})

  defp body(nil), do: []
  defp body({:json, term}), do: [json: term]
  defp body({:form, entries}), do: [form: entries(entries)]

  defp auth(nil), do: []
  defp auth({:basic, user, password}), do: [auth: {:basic, user <> ":" <> password}]

  # `extra` (Bubble's `returned_an_error`) goes into a JSON object.
  defp decode(_body, :empty, extra), do: {:ok, if(extra == %{}, do: nil, else: extra)}
  defp decode(body, :text, _extra), do: {:ok, body}

  defp decode(body, :json, extra) do
    with {:ok, term} <- json(body), do: {:ok, merge(term, extra)}
  end

  defp decode(body, {:json, decoder, list?}, extra) do
    with {:ok, term} <- json(body) do
      cond do
        is_nil(term) and extra == %{} -> {:ok, nil}
        list? and is_list(term) -> each(term, decoder)
        true -> decoder.(merge(term || %{}, extra))
      end
    end
  end

  defp merge(term, extra) when is_map(term), do: Map.merge(term, extra)
  defp merge(term, _extra), do: term

  defp json(""), do: {:ok, nil}

  defp json(body) do
    case Jason.decode(body) do
      {:ok, term} -> {:ok, term}
      {:error, error} -> {:error, {:decode, error}}
    end
  end

  defp each(items, decoder) do
    Enum.reduce_while(items, {:ok, []}, fn item, {:ok, acc} ->
      case decoder.(item) do
        {:ok, value} -> {:cont, {:ok, [value | acc]}}
        error -> {:halt, error}
      end
    end)
    |> case do
      {:ok, values} -> {:ok, Enum.reverse(values)}
      error -> error
    end
  end

  @doc false
  # A value as text for a URL, header or form: nil stays nil.
  @spec text(term()) :: String.t() | nil
  def text(nil), do: nil
  def text(value) when is_binary(value), do: value
  def text(value) when is_number(value) or is_atom(value), do: to_string(value)
  def text(value), do: Jason.encode!(value)

  @doc false
  # Values concatenated as text, a missing one as "".
  @spec string([term()]) :: String.t()
  def string(values), do: Enum.map_join(values, &(text(&1) || ""))

  @doc false
  # Values concatenated as text; nil when the only value is missing.
  @spec concat([term()]) :: String.t() | nil
  def concat([value]), do: text(value)
  def concat(values), do: string(values)

  @doc false
  # A value in a URL path segment, percent-encoded.
  @spec segment(term()) :: String.t()
  def segment(value) do
    case URI.encode(text(value) || "", &URI.char_unreserved?/1) do
      # `.` and `..` would move up the path (WTF-533).
      "." -> "%2E"
      ".." -> "%2E%2E"
      encoded -> encoded
    end
  end

  @doc false
  # A value in a host name.
  @spec host(term()) :: String.t()
  def host(value), do: URI.encode(text(value) || "", &(URI.char_unreserved?(&1) and &1 != ?~))

  @doc false
  # A JSON value from an environment variable: its JSON, else its text.
  @spec json_value(String.t()) :: term()
  def json_value(text) do
    case Jason.decode(text) do
      {:ok, term} -> term
      {:error, _} -> text
    end
  end

  @doc false
  # A parameter in a JSON body outside quotes (`"n": <n>`). Bubble pastes
  # its text into the body: a text that is a JSON number, yes/no or null
  # is that value; any other text (an object or array included, so a
  # page cannot send `{"$ne": null}`) is a JSON string. A list (the step's
  # value is a list) is a JSON array, any other value itself (WTF-533).
  @spec json_arg(term()) :: term()
  def json_arg(value) when is_binary(value) do
    case Jason.decode(value) do
      {:ok, scalar} when is_number(scalar) or is_boolean(scalar) or is_nil(scalar) -> scalar
      _ -> value
    end
  end

  def json_arg(value), do: value

  @doc false
  # A `Name: value` header line from an environment variable.
  @spec header_line(String.t()) :: {String.t(), String.t()}
  def header_line(line) do
    case String.split(line, ":", parts: 2) do
      [name, value] -> {String.trim(name), String.trim(value)}
      [value] -> {"authorization", String.trim(value)}
    end
  end

  @doc false
  # A `name=value` parameter from an environment variable.
  @spec param_line(String.t()) :: {String.t(), String.t()}
  def param_line(line) do
    case String.split(line, "=", parts: 2) do
      [name, value] -> {name, value}
      [name] -> {name, ""}
    end
  end

  @doc false
  # The value at a response path (keys and list indices), or nil.
  @spec at(term(), [String.t() | integer()]) :: term()
  def at(value, []), do: value
  def at(map, [key | rest]) when is_map(map) and is_binary(key), do: at(Map.get(map, key), rest)

  def at(list, [index | rest]) when is_list(list) and is_integer(index),
    do: at(Enum.at(list, index), rest)

  def at(_value, _path), do: nil

  @doc false
  # `fun` over a list; anything else is kept as is.
  @spec map_list(term(), (term() -> term())) :: term()
  def map_list(list, fun) when is_list(list), do: Enum.map(list, fun)
  def map_list(value, _fun), do: value

  @doc false
  # A response value as its API type's field type (a typed map, WTF-533):
  # Bubble's text, number and yes/no; a date from ISO 8601 text. Anything
  # it cannot read as that type is nil; other types stay as decoded.
  @spec cast_value(term(), atom() | {:list, atom()}) :: term()
  def cast_value(nil, _type), do: nil
  def cast_value(values, {:list, type}) when is_list(values), do: Enum.map(values, &cast_value(&1, type))
  def cast_value(_value, {:list, _type}), do: nil
  def cast_value(value, :text) when is_binary(value), do: value
  def cast_value(value, :text) when is_number(value) or is_boolean(value), do: to_string(value)
  def cast_value(value, :text), do: Jason.encode!(value)
  def cast_value(value, :number) when is_number(value), do: value

  def cast_value(value, :number) when is_binary(value) do
    case Float.parse(String.trim(value)) do
      {number, ""} -> number
      _ -> nil
    end
  end

  def cast_value(value, :boolean) when is_boolean(value), do: value
  def cast_value("true", :boolean), do: true
  def cast_value("false", :boolean), do: false

  def cast_value(value, :date) when is_binary(value) do
    case DateTime.from_iso8601(value) do
      {:ok, date, _offset} -> date
      {:error, _} -> nil
    end
  end

  def cast_value(_value, type) when type in [:number, :boolean, :date], do: nil
  def cast_value(value, _type), do: value

  @doc false
  # A typed struct from its fields.
  @spec cast(module(), term()) :: {:ok, struct()} | {:error, term()}
  def cast(module, fields) when is_map(fields) do
    case module.new(fields) do
      {:ok, value} -> {:ok, value}
      {:error, error} -> {:error, {:decode, error}}
    end
  end

  def cast(_module, _value), do: {:error, {:decode, :not_an_object}}
end
